{
 "id": "2025-xing-biophilic",
 "name": "Biophilic Interiors and Stress Recovery",
 "description": "An online stress-and-recovery experiment for building design. Xing et al. (2025, PLOS ONE) rendered a classroom, a corridor and a staircase at four levels of biophilic design: level 0 has no biophilic features; level 1 adds \"nature of the space\" (prospect and refuge, transparent walls, an open staircase); level 2 adds natural analogues (organic forms, nature-inspired artwork, green furniture); level 3 adds nature in the space (views of trees and sky, plants, green walls). Each of four rounds starts with a 20-second mental arithmetic test with unpleasant noise and a rigged comparison that always says the participant scored below average. Participants then rate eight mood words (1 = not at all, 5 = very much), view the three interiors of one level for 10 seconds each, rate the mood words again and say whether they liked the designs (−2 to +2). The mood words pair up into four theories: stress recovery (relaxed / irritable), attention restoration (attentive / fatigued), refuge and prospect (self-assured / frightened) and inspiration (inspired / downhearted). For each pair the paper computes mood change: (positive item + reversed negative item) after viewing minus the same sum after the stress test. 255 participants took part. Level 0 lowered mood and each added level raised it, with the largest gains for recovery and inspiration. The page records the change scores per level as biophilic_scores. Analysis as in the paper: a repeated-measures ANOVA of level (0–3) for each theory, with Bonferroni post-hoc tests. The template ships with the paper's 12 renderings (Fig 2, CC BY 4.0), cropped from the figure: classroom_level0.jpg … staircase_level3.jpg. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper does not report the order of the four levels; the template randomises it per participant; (2) the paper's arithmetic items are shown as completed equations (e.g. 373 + 386 = 759), and the template asks whether each equation is correct (about half are wrong); the paper does not say how answers were given; (3) the traffic and construction recordings are replaced by noise the page synthesises (a low rumble and hammering bursts), so no audio files are needed; (4) the images are the small figure versions (about 550 × 330 pixels), not the original renderings; (5) the paper asked about the living environment in and outside term time, and the template asks only about growing up and living now; (6) the page tells participants at the end that the comparison was not real; (7) the paper's survey ran in Qualtrics and Gorilla.",
